# Line-ending and text policy.
#
# Without this, every checkout re-normalises files and `git status` reports
# whole-file diffs on a machine whose autocrlf setting differs from the one
# that committed the file - which is what the repeated
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings were telling us.
#
# `text=auto` lets git store LF in the repository and hand the working tree
# whatever its native convention is (CRLF on Windows, LF elsewhere). The
# per-type rules below are the cases where that default is wrong.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell and Python: a CRLF here breaks the shebang and the interpreter on any
# POSIX machine, and on Windows Git Bash refuses a script with CRLF.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# Windows-only launchers must stay CRLF: cmd.exe and PowerShell mangle a
# batch file whose lines end in LF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Rust, TypeScript, Vue and the rest are handled by `text=auto eol=lf`;
# they are listed only to document that the choice is deliberate.
*.rs text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.vue text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf

# Binaries: never touch their bytes or git will report them as modified on
# every checkout and `git diff` will try to render them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.bin binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary

# Lockfiles are generated: keep them out of diffs and merge summaries.
pnpm-lock.yaml -diff
apps/desktop/src-tauri/Cargo.lock -diff

# Test and build output are not part of the code a reviewer reads.
playwright-report/** linguist-generated
test-results/** linguist-generated

